WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.120
Jean-Luc Picard is back for a new adventure
in Star Trek: Picard, once again boldly going

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.500
where no one has gone before.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:11.480
The CBS All Access show is filled with callbacks
to Star Trek past, including some that might

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.960
elude even the most dedicated of fans.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:19.280
After opening with a flashback to Picard's
resignation from Starfleet 14 years earlier,

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.630
the third episode of Picard, titled "The End
is the Beginning," finds the erstwhile Admiral

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:28.130
paying a visit to his former first officer,
Raffi, who was fired from Starfleet after

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.160
Picard resigned.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.040
Raffi is now living in a self-described "hovel"
in the Vasquez Rocks, where it seems she passes

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.060
her time getting high and trying to forget
the years she spent working alongside Picard.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.720
However, for long-time Star Trek fans, the
Vasquez Rocks are more than just a cool location

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.000
for Raffi to park her 24th century trailer.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.740
The Vasquez Rocks Natural Area Park, located
in the Sierra Pelona Mountains in northern

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:54.820
California, has been a frequent shooting location
for past Star Trek productions, including

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.290
episodes of the original series, The Next
Generation, Voyager, and Enterprise, along

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:04.360
with scenes in the films Star Trek IV: The
Voyage Home, J.J. Abrams' 2009 Star Trek,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.640
and its 2013 sequel, Star Trek Into Darkness.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.700
As a matter of fact, the Vasquez Rocks have
been featured so prominently within Star Trek

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.040
lore that the location has even been nicknamed
"Kirk's Rock."

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.520
When Picard meets his new pilot-for-hire,
Cristóbal "Chris" Rios, it's under less than

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:25.810
ideal circumstances, as Rios is in the process
of having a piece of titanium shrapnel removed

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:26.620
from his shoulder.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.500
In order for the Emergency Medical Hologram
to access the injury, Rios is shirtless, giving

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.260
us a clear view at the prominent mermaid tattoo
on his upper left arm.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:39.900
Rios' ship is named the La Sirena, which translates
from Spanish as "The Mermaid."

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.800
It makes sense that Rios would have such a
strong personal connection to his ship that

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:47.810
he'd want to permanently mark it on his body,
but his choice to name his ship "The Mermaid"

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:52.140
could also be a hint that Rios and Picard
may have more in common than it might appear.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.180
"Do I detect a certain bitterness toward Starfleet?"

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.100
Mermaids and mermaid tattoos have long been
associated with a romanticized image of sailors

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.540
heading out on perilous voyages, which is
how Picard often seemed to view his role as

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.620
the commander of a Federation starship.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.630
This was made plain by the opening of Star
Trek: Generations, in which Picard and the

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:16.310
crew of the Enterprise stepped into their
equivalent roles as officers on a 19th century

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:16.800
naval ship.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.140
"Just imagine what it was like.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.760
No engines, no computers — just the wind
and the sea and the stars to guide you."

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:30.540
Rios' embracing of such a classic maritime
symbol may indicate that despite their apparent

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.840
differences, Rios and Picard may in fact be
kindred spirits.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.840
After they meet, Picard notices that Rios
is in the middle of reading a hardcover version

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:41.560
of The Tragic Sense of Life by Miguel de Unamuno.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.700
Written in 1912, it's a philosophical essay
following the author's struggle to reconcile

00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:52.160
man's pursuit of wisdom, knowledge, and reason
with his belief that those pursuits and consciousness

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.260
in general actually work in opposition to
leading a happy life.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.400
The particular passage that Rios is reading
later on in the episode, on page 140 of the

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.000
book, deals with the link between consciousness
and suffering, in which Unamuno muses,

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.040
"How do we know that we exist if we do not
suffer, little or much?

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.920
How can we turn upon ourselves, acquire reflective
consciousness, save by suffering?"

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.020
While Picard hasn't been explicitly shown
to be a fan of this author, it's well established

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.120
that he's a learned, philosophical man.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.520
Rios' interest in philosophy is yet another
clue that the pilot may have more in common

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.150
with the Admiral than meets the eye.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:30.530
But the passage Rios was reading also seems
to point somewhat ominously to the themes

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:34.260
of sentience and personhood that so strongly
define Picard.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.480
Unamuno argues that suffering is essential
to consciousness, and that without it, we

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.450
have no way of knowing that we are truly alive
a philosophy that we may see revisited as

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.170
we learn more about Dahj and Soji's origins,
and the circumstances leading up to the synthetic

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:48.660
uprising.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:53.360
Despite Raffi's insistence that she wants
no part of Picard's new mission, he sends

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.830
her all of the information he has on Bruce
Maddox's research anyway, and she can't help

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:58.460
digging into it.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.620
As Raffi sifts through the files from the
Daystrom Institute, there's a brief shot of

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.190
her screen, on which the words "Crypto Algorithm
Identification: Gorn Egg" can be read.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:13.360
The Gorn are a reptilian alien species first
introduced on the classic Trek episode, "Arena,"

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.660
in which Captain Kirk is transported to the
surface of a rocky planet filmed at the Vasquez

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:22.309
Rocks by aliens called the Metrons, and told
he must battle the captain of an opposing

00:04:22.309 --> 00:04:23.590
Gorn ship to the death.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.680
Only the winner's ship and crew will survive.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.330
Kirk and the Gorn captain both manage to injure
one another, but Kirk ultimately decides to

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:35.080
show mercy and spare the Gorn's life, which
in turn leads to both crews being spared by

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.080
the Metrons.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.500
"No I won't kill him.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:39.800
Do you hear?

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.580
You'll have to get your entertainment someplace
else."

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.460
At the end of "Arena," the Metrons tell Kirk
that while humans are a savage race, there

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:52.140
is hope for them due to their capacity to
show mercy to their enemies.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.020
Conversely, the beginning of Picard's third
episode finds Picard and Raffi lamenting that

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:01.849
the Federation is refusing to show mercy to
the Romulans, capitulating instead to "intolerance

00:05:01.849 --> 00:05:02.520
and fear."

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.240
Had Kirk given into those same tendencies
in "Arena," it would've led to a far different

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.540
and more disastrous outcome.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.159
Despite Rios' many personal similarities to
Picard, the pilot is initially reluctant to

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.780
agree to help the former Starfleet Admiral
when they first meet.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.120
Later, when Rios is alone in his quarters,
the Emergency Medical Hologram appears and

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.720
attempts to talk Rios into helping Picard,
despite his reservations.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.060
"So, are we excited?

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:27.960
Intimidated?

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.900
Maybe a teensy bit starstruck?"

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:35.620
As the EMH runs down his laundry list of Picard's
accomplishments, he mentions that Picard was

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:40.389
"chief contact with the Q continuum," which
is a reference to both the pilot episode and

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:45.020
series finale of Star Trek: The Next Generation,
as well as a number of notable episodes during

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:45.780
the series run.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.060
Picard's encounters with Q also led to the
first introduction of, and subsequent attacks

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:50.960
by the Borg.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.220
He also mentions that Picard was "Arbiter
of Succession for the Klingon Empire," which

00:05:55.229 --> 00:05:59.779
was already nodded to in Picard's premiere
episode, and that he was "captain of the Enterprises

00:05:59.779 --> 00:06:02.929
D and E," a reference that needs no explanation.

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:07.149
The EMH wraps up his exultations of Picard
by name-dropping that he "worked alongside

00:06:07.149 --> 00:06:11.529
the great Spock," calling back to the Next
Generation episode "Unification," and could

00:06:11.529 --> 00:06:16.789
also be a nod to Spock's role in attempting
to prevent the Romulan supernova, as explained

00:06:16.789 --> 00:06:18.220
in 2009's Star Trek.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.120
While most of Picard's storyline takes place
on Earth, all of Dahj's twin sister Soji's

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:28.199
scenes have been set aboard a Romulan reclamation
station on a repurposed Borg cube, on which

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.880
she encounters an ex-Borg drone named Hugh.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.069
Hugh first appeared in the Next Generation
episode "I Borg", which saw the Enterprise

00:06:35.069 --> 00:06:39.020
crew encounter a young Borg drone who had
been abandoned by the Collective.

00:06:39.409 --> 00:06:43.440
Although Picard initially sees the Borg as
nothing more than a way to help him bring

00:06:43.449 --> 00:06:47.569
down the Collective, he ultimately is forced
to recognize his sentience and personhood

00:06:47.569 --> 00:06:52.600
when the drone begins exhibiting signs of
individuality and gives himself the name "Hugh."

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:56.700
"I will not assist you."

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:57.800
"'I?'"

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.159
Hugh pops up again in the two-part TNG episode
"Descent," where it is revealed that after

00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:08.240
rejoining the Collective, his sense of individuality
spread to his entire cube, and they were cut

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:09.300
off from the Collective.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.680
In "Descent," Hugh proved himself a loyal
ally of Picard and the Enterprise, rescuing

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.280
several members of the crew and resisting
Data's megalomaniacal brother, Lore.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:19.900
"You're kind of a know-it-all, aren't you."

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:24.020
It's hard to predict what's in store for Hugh
on Picard, but if Hugh's past history with

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:28.869
the Admiral are any indication, he may prove
to be an important albeit unexpected ally

00:07:28.869 --> 00:07:30.660
for Picard and his rogue crew.

